I also feel that the non-identifiability is one of the main reasons for running slow. In this model. the intercept a, and the beta's are non-identifiable, which results in the high correlation between the beta's in the same category, as is shown by Jiqiang.

When I was doing the BDA assignments, I will usually drop one of beta's in order to get an identifiable model. For example, I will drop beta_sex for male and only use beta_sex for female. I do know that by doing this I introduce some kind of asymmetry, but at least it runs fast...

If this kind of non-identifiability is desirable in order to preserve the symmetric form, I definitely believe that a good estimation of cov matrix will improve NUTS greatly. Also RMHMC may be powerful in this case.

