On 1/17/13 5:28 PM, Nigel/essence wrote:
> ps the problems I tackle can be up to 1000 dimensions, have up to 5000 data points.

Back of the envelope, this is near the top of what we could expect
Stan to do in terms of matrix ops and gradients.

A naive implementation would take ~ 1K * 5K * 5K) ops to construct
the covar matrix, then ~ (5K)^3 for the Cholesky factorization.

> More commonly I have 50 dimensions and 400 data points. Hence constructing K is a bottleneck.

Right -- that's about what Rasmussen and Williams (or maybe Neal)
were saying -- that whether it's the gradient calc or the
matrix or solving will depend on the exact dimensions of the problem.

- Bob

